  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Public Expenditure and Financial Accountability Initiative PEFA Phase VI</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The PEFA program provides a framework for assessing and reporting on the strengths and weaknesses of public financial management (PFM) using quantitative indicators to measure performance. PEFA assessments at country level is used by most of the governments in partner countries as an important input to design and monitor PFM-reforms (tax and revenue administrations, fiscal and expenditure management and oversight functions). The Public Expenditure and Financial Accountability (PEFA) program was initiated in 2001 by seven international development partners: The European Commission, International Monetary Fund, World Bank, and the governments of France, Norway, Switzerland, and the United Kingdom. The agreement is to support a phase VI of the initiative. 

Country reviews: Kenya, Nepal, Uganda, Angola, Mozambique (sub-nationals), Moldova (3 sub-nationals), Malawi, Mozambique, Tanzania, Ukraine and planned for Palestina, Colombia, Indonesia, DR Kongo, Somalia, Ethiopia, Niger and more.</narrative>
  </description>
